< q83
Breast cancer has 30 features. How many PCA components do you need to keep 95% of the variance? Use `PCA(n_components=0.95)` after scaling and print the number of components. Then try without scaling and explain the difference.
< h83
A number between 0 and 1 for `n_components` means "keep this share of variance"; the result is in `.n_components_`
< a83
from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer
from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA

X, y = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)
scaled = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=0.95)).fit(X)
print("scaled:  ", scaled[-1].n_components_)    # about 10
raw = PCA(n_components=0.95).fit(X)
print("unscaled:", raw.n_components_)           # 1!
# Without scaling, "area" (values up to ~2500) has by far the biggest spread, so PCA
# thinks one column holds almost all the information. Scale before PCA.
